    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d645">

                <pb facs="0509" n="501" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg501"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.115 Panay/70: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commander in Chief of the United States Asiatic
                        Fleet</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Yarnell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Chief of Naval
                        Operations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Leahy</hi>)<anchor n="79" xml:id="d645-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-15T10:10:00+08:00">December 15, 1937—10:10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 15—1 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>0015. H.M.S. <hi rendition="#italic">Bee</hi> reports first group <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Panay</hi> survivors arrived 2350 Tuesday, remainder at
                    0200 today. Two coffins will be brought out at 0900 today. As far as can be
                    learned all persons accounted for. Following stretcher cases: Lt. Comdr. J. J.
                    Hughes, Lt. A. F. Anders, K. J. Rice (E.M. 3d), J. H. Lang (C.Q.M.), C. H. Birk
                    (E.M. 1st), A. A. Kozak (M.M. 2d), E. W. G. Hulsebus (Cox’n), P. D. Ziegler <pb
                        facs="0510" n="502" xml:id="pg502"/> (S.C. 3d), C. S. Schroyer (Sea’n 1st),
                    N. L. Davis (F 1st), Mr. Gassie, Embassy clerk. MacDonald,<anchor n="80"
                        xml:id="d645-f2"/> correspondent, among first group survivors, reports <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Panay</hi> hit about 1350 12th by bomb from great
                    height. Ship abandoned at 1405. Before vessel sank was machine gunned by two
                    Japanese Army motor boats whose crews boarded about 1554 and left before vessel
                    sank. Colors were flying and clearly visible during whole time. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Oahu</hi> radio was out of commission 10 hours, now back
                    in commission.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
